'''Giorgis Nuur''' (born 3 November 1953) is a Burawi politician and former seminarian who has served as the founding [[Leader of the Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Leader of the Christian Socialist Union]] since 1996. He has represented [[Qeturaga-3]] in the [[Lower House of Burawa]] since the party's formation. A former member of the [[National Party of Burawa]], Nuur led the faction that split from the party on 5 April 1996 in opposition to the [[Domestic Partnership Act of 1996 (Burawa)|Domestic Partnership Act of 1996]], establishing the Christian Socialist Union as a voice for Catholic social conservatism in Burawi politics.

Nuur studied theology at the [[University of Ampuria]] in [[Volisania]] and trained for the priesthood at the [[Seminary of St. Thomas]] before entering politics. His religious background has shaped his political advocacy for Catholic social teaching, protectionism, and opposition to economic liberalisation.

He is the Christian Socialist Union candidate in the [[2026 Burawi snap leadership election|2026 snap leadership election]].

== Early life and education ==

Giorgis Nuur was born on 3 November 1953 in [[Qeturaga]], a city in the [[Central Burawa]] region. He was raised in a devout [[Burawi Catholic Church|Catholic]] household; his family were regular communicants and active in parish life. Qeturaga's Catholic community, one of the largest in Burawa, provided the religious environment that would shape his political identity.

Nuur completed his secondary education in Qeturaga before pursuing religious studies abroad. He enrolled at the [[University of Ampuria]] in the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Volisania]], where he studied theology. He subsequently entered the [[Seminary of St. Thomas]] to train for the priesthood.

After completing his seminary formation, Nuur discerned that his vocation lay in lay political service rather than ordained ministry. He returned to Burawa in the late 1970s and entered political life, bringing to his work the theological formation and moral framework of Catholic social teaching.

== Early political career ==

=== National Party membership (1977–1996) ===

At age 24, Nuur joined the [[National Party of Burawa]], drawn by the party's appeal to rural and Catholic voters and its conservative socialist platform. The National Party, rooted in the merger of the [[Christian Socialist Party of Burawa]] and the [[Tenant Farmers Party of Burawa]] in the 1980s, combined social conservatism with agrarian economic priorities.

Nuur served as a local party organiser in Qeturaga, building networks among Catholic parishes and farming communities. He represented the conservative wing of the party, advocating for the protection of traditional values and the interests of rural Catholic communities.

=== The 1996 split ===

{{main|Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)}}

In 1996, the National Party voted in favour of the [[Domestic Partnership Act of 1996 (Burawa)|Domestic Partnership Act]], which recognised civil partnerships for same-sex couples. The legislation generated intense opposition from socially conservative National Party members, particularly those representing rural Catholic communities.

On 5 April 1996, Nuur led a faction of conservative National Party members in a formal split from the party. The breakaway group established the '''Christian Socialist Union'''{{mdash}}a new party committed to Catholic social teaching, protectionism, and opposition to social liberalism. Nuur was elected as the party's founding leader.

The split was acrimonious. The National Party accused the defectors of betraying party unity, while the newly formed CSU characterised the National Party as having abandoned its conservative principles. In the subsequent election, the CSU attracted significant support in rural Catholic communities, particularly in [[Sago]] and the [[Dafar]] region, at the National Party's electoral expense.

== Leadership of the Christian Socialist Union ==

As founding leader of the Christian Socialist Union, Nuur has defined the party's platform around Catholic social teaching, social conservatism, and economic protectionism. The CSU has positioned itself as the defender of traditional values within Burawa's socialist framework, opposing social liberalism while supporting mainstream socialist economic policy.

=== Electoral performance ===

The CSU has maintained a stable parliamentary presence since its formation, drawing support from rural communities with strong Catholic congregations. The party holds 5 Senate seats and 60 Lower House seats, concentrated in regions with significant Catholic populations.

Nuur has represented [[Qeturaga-3]] since 1996, consistently winning re-election as the CSU incumbent. The constituency encompasses the Catholic neighbourhoods of his birthplace and reflects the party's base among religious voters in Central Burawa.

=== Relationship with the National Party ===

Despite the bitterness of the 1996 split, the CSU and National Party have developed an informal conservative alliance in the decades since. Both parties share positions on social conservatism and agrarian protectionism, and they frequently cooperate on votes involving traditional values and rural interests.

The alliance was tested during the [[2026 Burawi leadership recall vote]], when the CSU voted against the recall of National Party Leader [[Hancil Thea]]. The party's support for Thea reflected both the conservative alliance and opposition to what Nuur characterised as "metropolitan overreach" against a rural Leader.

=== 2016 same-sex marriage vote ===

{{main|Same-sex marriage in Burawa}}

In 2016, the Forward Party government of [[Marik Sahamin]] passed legislation recognising same-sex marriage. Nuur led CSU opposition to the legislation, characterising it as a violation of Catholic teaching and traditional Burawi values. The party's opposition was unsuccessful, but the vote reinforced the CSU's identity as the institutional voice of Catholic social conservatism.

== 2026 snap election campaign ==

{{main|2026 Burawi snap election}}

Following the successful recall of [[Hancil Thea]] and the scheduling of snap elections, Nuur announced his candidacy for [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] as the Christian Socialist Union nominee. His campaign emphasised Catholic social teaching, protectionism, opposition to economic liberalisation, and the defence of rural and traditional communities.

Nuur participated in televised debates on 8 April and 12 April 2026, alongside the other candidates. His polling performance placed him behind the major candidates, reflecting the CSU's role as a niche party representing Catholic social conservatism rather than a broad-based political movement.

== Political positions ==

Nuur's political positions are grounded in [[Catholic social teaching]] and the platform of the Christian Socialist Union.

On social issues, Nuur opposes same-sex marriage, abortion, and what he characterises as the "moral decay" of secular liberalism. He supported the Domestic Partnership Act's repeal and opposed the 2016 same-sex marriage legislation. He advocates for policies that support traditional family structures and Catholic education.

On economic policy, Nuur advocates protectionism, opposition to economic liberalisation, and support for state ownership of essential industries. He has criticised the Industrial Party's push for corporate tax reduction and opposes privatisation of state enterprises.

On foreign policy, Nuur supports Burawa's traditional non-interventionist stance and has opposed military operations such as the [[2022 Sabia operation]].

On regional policy, Nuur advocates for rural investment and the protection of agricultural communities against what he characterises as metropolitan neglect. He has defended the Dhulsarren against the HSR Scandal sanctions, arguing that collective punishment violates principles of justice.

== Personal life ==

Nuur is a [[Burawi Catholic Church|Catholic]] layperson. He pursued religious formation as a seminarian but discerned against ordination. He is unmarried and has no children, a personal situation he has attributed to his decades of dedication to political and religious service.

He resides in [[Qeturaga]] and remains active in parish life.

